KILL(2)                        P32W Unix                         KILL(2)

NAME
       kill, raise - send a signal to a process

SYNOPSIS
       #include <signal.h>

       int kill(int pid, int sig);
       int raise(int sig);

DESCRIPTION
       kill sends the signal sig (see signal(2)) to the process pid.
       The super-user may signal any process; another user only a
       process whose real user ID equals the sender's real or
       effective user ID. With sig 0 nothing is sent, but the
       checks are made: a way to ask whether a process exists.

       If the process ignores sig, nothing happens (SIGKILL cannot
       be ignored). A process waiting in pause(2), wait(2) or on a
       terminal or pipe is woken, and that call fails with EINTR if
       the process survives the signal.

       raise(sig) is the library's kill(getpid(), sig).

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ESRCH   No process (other than a zombie) has the ID pid.
       EPERM   The caller may not signal that process.

SEE ALSO
       signal(2), kill(1), ps(1)

BUGS
       pid must name one process: 0, -1 and negative process groups
       are not supported (there are no process groups), and give
       ESRCH. A sig outside 0 to 15 is silently ignored rather than
       refused with EINVAL. A process has room for one pending
       signal; a second one sent before the first is acted on
       replaces it.
